@CarpenterBlue Forgive me if I'm misunderstanding, but I believe using a custom TextureBlit Shader with your Drawable Texture should allow you to perform the desired behavior?

```glsl
shader_type texture_blit;
render_mode blend_sub;

uniform sampler2D source_texture : hint_blit_source;

void blit() {
	vec4 sample = texture(source_texture, UV);
	if (sample == vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)){
		COLOR = vec4(0, 0, 0, 1.0);
	} else {
		COLOR = vec4(0, 0, 0, 0.0);
	}
}
```

This samples the source texture, and if the source's 'pixel' is black, outputs Color(0, 0, 0, 1.0) which subtracts from the targets original alpha because the Blit Shader is set to Blend_Mode_Sub. Else outputs Color(0, 0, 0, 0.0) which has no change.


https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d52f4371-61e3-4e3f-ba66-92121515dfd9

Attached video of this shader in motion.

TWO NOTES:
1. Blend Mode Sub does not appear to do a direct subtraction on Alpha, so while outputting Alpha 0.5 will reduce the alpha of the target, it won't reduce it to exactly 0.5 and then 0 if done twice?
2. Blend Mode Sub does not operate on Alpha in the GL_Compatibility renderer, perhaps related to [this issue](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/29105)

EDIT: Disregard second note, Pushed a fix, 8/6/25, Blend Modes appropriately interact with Alpha in GL_Compatibility now.

Got some clarification from @KoBeWi and have pushed several fixes.

> Crash when you try to blit with null source texture

Should be resolved, Blit_rect now has better Null handling for textures. (as well as a little code cleanup for better readability and consistency in logic order between the Forward+ and GL renderers)

> The material argument seems to have no effect.
> Looks like there is no way to blend a rect?

These were non-issues, both features work when using a ShaderMaterial with the new ShaderType Texture_blit and its blend modes.

> The modulate argument could be optional and default to white.

Done!

> Blitting a GradientTexture2D does not work [...] EDIT: Looks like you have to wait for it to initialize first. It would be nice to at least get some error if a texture is used too early.

Appears to be outside of the scope of this pull request, the textures & placeholders are behaving exactly as intended.
```
RID GradientTexture2D::get_rid() const {
	if (!texture.is_valid()) {
		texture = RS::get_singleton()->texture_2d_placeholder_create();
	}
	return texture;
}
```
If we wanted to print an error when an RID request returns a placeholder instead of the texture, that would have to be inside the get_rid() function of every Texture2D child. I'm unsure if there's a way for my function receiving that RID to detect if it's a placeholder, and I don't think that would be desirable. It does appear live loading a gradient in the same GDScript function as the blit call doesn't give the Gradient time to initialize, but pre-loading the Gradient in the GDScript file works correctly.

Edit: Another push - added very rough docs and fixed something else to better pass Github Actions checks.

